"text":" as OA"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"S, GI"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"S, or the Can"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ada Child"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" Benefit. |"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" Withdrawals count"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" as income and can affect those"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" benefits. |"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"\n\n**Which"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" to pr"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ioritize**"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"\n- **R"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"RSP:"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"** It"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" t"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ends to fav"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"our"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" people"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" with a"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" higher income now"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" who"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" expect a"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" lower t"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ax rate in retirement."},{"type":"text_delta","text":" You"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" get the"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" deduction at"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" a high rate and pay t"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ax later"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" at a lower one"},{"type":"text_delta","text":".\n- **TFSA"},{"type":"text_delta","text":":** It tends to favour"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" people with a lower income now,"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" or people"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" who want"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" flexible access"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" to"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" the"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" money. It works"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" well for em"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ergency funds,"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" medium-term goals, and"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" retirement savings.\n-"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" **"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"Both"},{"type":"text_delta","text":":** Many people use both."},{"type":"text_delta","text":" A"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" common appro"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ach is to use the RRS"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"P refund to top"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" up a TFSA."},{"type":"text_delta","text":"\n\nAnnual limits and r"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ules change,"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" so check"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" the current"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" fig"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ures on your"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" CRA My"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" Account. I can"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" look"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" up this"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" year's limits if you'"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"d like."},{"type":"completed","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"opaque","provider":"anthropic","kind":"thinking","data":{"signature":"","thinking":"","type":"thinking"}},{"type":"text","text":"**TFSA (Tax-Free Savings Account) vs. RRSP (Registered Retirement Savings Plan)**\n\n| | **TFSA** | **RRSP** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Tax on contributions** | Made with after-tax money, so there is no deduction. | Tax-deductible, so they reduce your taxable income for the year. |\n| **Tax on growth** | Tax-free. | Tax-deferred. |\n| **Tax on withdrawals** | None. Withdrawals aren't taxed. | Taxed as income when you withdraw. |\n| **Contribution room** | A fixed annual amount for everyone 18 or older. Unused room carries forward. | 18% of your previous year's earned income, up to an annual cap. Unused room carries forward. |\n| **Withdrawals** | Flexible. You can withdraw any time, and the withdrawn amount is added back to your room the next calendar year. | Withdrawals are taxed, and the room is generally lost. Exceptions are the Home Buyers' Plan and the Lifelong Learning Plan, which let you borrow from your RRSP and repay it. |\n| **Age limit** | None for contributing, as long as you're 18 or older. | You must convert it to a RRIF or annuity by the end of the year you turn 71. |\n| **Effect on benefits** | Withdrawals don't affect income-tested benefits such as OAS, GIS, or the Canada Child Benefit. | Withdrawals count as income and can affect those benefits. |\n\n**Which to prioritize**\n- **RRSP:** It tends to favour people with a higher income now who expect a lower tax rate in retirement. You get the deduction at a high rate and pay tax later at a lower one.\n- **TFSA:** It tends to favour people with a lower income now, or people who want flexible access to the money. It works well for emergency funds, medium-term goals, and retirement savings.\n- **Both:** Many people use both. A common approach is to use the RRSP refund to top up a TFSA.\n\nAnnual limits and rules change, so check the current figures on your CRA My Account. I can look up this year's limits if you'd like."}]},"stop_reason":"end_turn","usage":{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":798,"cache_read_input_tokens":4607,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0},"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","refusal_category":null}]}
